{
  "moduleId": "m00244",
  "title": {
    "segmentId": "m00244:title:auto-1",
    "text": "Chemistry of Esters"
  },
  "documentClass": null,
  "metadata": {
    "uuid": "281f9ca7-a3fa-40e3-ba87-5576a4a60163"
  },
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00001",
      "segmentId": "m00244:para:para-00001"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00001",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00244:alt:fig-00001-alt",
          "text": "The first structure shows methyl butanoate, the second shows isopentyl acetate, and the third shows a fat molecule where R denotes carbon chain-length varying from eleven to seventeen."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_21_06_001.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00002",
      "segmentId": "m00244:para:para-00002"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00002",
      "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00244:alt:fig-00002-alt",
          "text": "The structure of dibutyl phthalate shows a benzene ring with two carbonyl groups ortho to each other. The carbonyl groups are each attached to butoxy groups."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_21_06_002.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00001",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00244:title:sect-00001-title",
        "text": "Preparation of Esters"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00003",
          "segmentId": "m00244:para:para-00003"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00003",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00244:alt:fig-00003-alt",
              "text": "The flow chart shows conversion of carboxylic acids to esters. The first example uses sodium hydroxide and an alkyl halide, the second uses an alcohol and acid, and the third uses thionyl chloride, followed by an alcohol and pyridine."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_21_06_003.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00002",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00244:title:sect-00002-title",
        "text": "Reactions of Esters"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00004",
          "segmentId": "m00244:para:para-00004"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00004",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00244:alt:fig-00004-alt",
              "text": "The structure shows a lactone (cyclic ester) in which an oxygen atom replaces carbon in a cyclohexane ring. The carbon atom adjacent to oxygen is double-bonded to oxygen."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_21_06_004.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "section",
          "id": "sect-00003",
          "class": null,
          "title": {
            "segmentId": "m00244:title:sect-00003-title",
            "text": "Conversion of Esters into Carboxylic Acids: Hydrolysis"
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00005",
              "segmentId": "m00244:para:para-00005"
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00005",
              "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
              "caption": null,
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00244:alt:fig-00005-alt",
                  "text": "The lactone structure comprises of a six-membered ring containing a carbonyl group with an oxygen atom next to it in the ring."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_21_06_005.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00006",
              "segmentId": "m00244:para:para-00006"
            },
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00007",
              "segmentId": "m00244:para:para-00007"
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00006",
              "class": "mechanism-figure scaled-down",
              "caption": {
                "segmentId": "m00244:caption:fig-00006-caption"
              },
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00244:alt:fig-00006-alt",
                  "text": "The curly arrow mechanism of saponification shows the four steps involved in forming a carboxylic acid from an ester in the presence of hydroxide ions."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_21_06_006.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00008",
              "segmentId": "m00244:para:para-00008"
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00007",
              "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
              "caption": null,
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00244:alt:fig-00007-alt",
                  "text": "The reaction shows an ester (ethyl propionate) reacting with aqueous sodium hydroxide followed by treatment with acid to give propanoic acid and ethanol."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_21_06_007.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00009",
              "segmentId": "m00244:para:para-00009"
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00008",
              "class": "mechanism-figure scaled-down",
              "caption": {
                "segmentId": "m00244:caption:fig-00008-caption"
              },
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00244:alt:fig-00008-alt",
                  "text": "The curly arrow mechanism for the acid-catalyzed hydrolysis of an ester shows the four steps involved in forming a carboxylic acid from an ester and hydronium ions."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_21_06_008.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00010",
              "segmentId": "m00244:para:para-00010"
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00009",
              "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
              "caption": null,
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00244:alt:fig-00009-alt",
                  "text": "The reaction shows a fat reacting with an enzyme and base to form a fatty acid and regenerating the enzyme. The reaction involves tetrahedral intermediates, shown in parentheses. The curly arrow mechanism is also shown"
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_21_06_009.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "section",
          "id": "sect-00004",
          "class": "section-exercises",
          "title": null,
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "exercise",
