              "problem": {
                "content": []
              },
              "solution": null
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "section",
          "id": "sect-00005",
          "class": null,
          "title": {
            "segmentId": "m00244:title:sect-00005-title",
            "text": "Conversion of Esters into Amides: Aminolysis"
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00012",
              "segmentId": "m00244:para:para-00012"
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00010",
              "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
              "caption": null,
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00244:alt:fig-00010-alt",
                  "text": "Methyl benzoate reacts with ammonia in ether to form benzamide and methanol. The benzamide structure has a benzene ring attached to the amide (C O N H 2) group."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_21_06_010.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "section",
          "id": "sect-00006",
          "class": null,
          "title": {
            "segmentId": "m00244:title:sect-00006-title",
            "text": "Conversion of Esters into Alcohols: Reduction"
          },
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00013",
              "segmentId": "m00244:para:para-00013"
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00011",
              "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
              "caption": null,
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00244:alt:fig-00011-alt",
                  "text": "Ethyl-2-pentenoate is converted to 2-penten-1-ol in ninety-two percent yield when treated with lithium aluminum hydride followed by acid. A five-membered lactone is converted to 1,4-pentanediol in eighty-six percent yield using the same reagents."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_21_06_011.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00014",
              "segmentId": "m00244:para:para-00014"
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00012",
              "class": "unnumbered",
              "caption": null,
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00244:alt:fig-00012-alt",
                  "text": "The curly arrow mechanism for the lithium aluminum hydride reduction of an ester to an alcohol is shown. A tetrahedral alkoxide and an aldehyde are formed as intermediates."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_21_06_012.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            },
            {
              "type": "para",
              "id": "para-00015",
              "segmentId": "m00244:para:para-00015"
            },
            {
              "type": "figure",
              "id": "fig-00013",
              "class": "unnumbered",
              "caption": null,
              "media": {
                "alt": {
                  "segmentId": "m00244:alt:fig-00013-alt",
                  "text": "The conversion of ethyl dodecanoate to dodecanal (eighty-eight percent) and ethanol using D I B A H, a reagent with two isobutyl groups single-bonded to a central aluminum atom single-bonded to a hydrogen atom."
                },
                "src": "../../media/OChem_21_06_013.jpg",
                "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
